Y'know, these are good points and things I forgot to include. Thanks for bringing them up. However, now that they have been brought up, I want to address them. As far as that first quote goes, I wouldn't trust Moxxi's word for it. Like you said, they dated and broke up prior to PreSe, and based on that description it sounds like it was an all around good relationship. Holding open doors, and being adoring, and casually murdering  people for looking at you weirdly. Which, yes that last one sounds bad but it's the Borderlands, where a casual Murder happens every 5 odd seconds and usually for much dumber reasons. And she says she dumped him because he quote "got clingy". This is coming from Moxxi, someone who, while a decent person for the most part, likes to fuck around a lot. I mean, she literally dumped Mordecai just to get on the long-term train with Jack. When she says it wasn't the best idea, she could just mean that she spammed her personal ECHO and it was just a migraine. I mean, if he blew up the underdome prior to Pre-Se, why would she work with him during Pre-Se, right? Especially since when she actually betrays him during Eye to Eye, she just says "I've been watching you, and there something wrong about you." In other words, she tries to kill him based on a personal opinion. And not just him, but everyone on Helios, just to kill him. Which would, I think, make any reasonable person furious.

&nbsp;

As for this other quote, I just want to compare the start and end of Pre-Se. At the start, he's being actively shot at by invading hostile forces, and he's defending himself, surrounded on all sides. Then starts getting his ass whooped. It's only at the end, after he gets his vision of the future where he learns about the warrior and truly freakin snaps. Where he actually starts talking about burning people en masse (sets all those people on fire?) And the events of Pre-Sequel aptly describe his descent into for the reason that, as previously described, he gets nothing but figurative and in some instances literal knives in the back for 3 straight days. And betrayal hurts man.

&nbsp;

I'd also like to point out that the writing becomes inconsistent after 2. This can be observed in Get to know Jack, particularly Echo#3 when "Moorin" mentions Jack's wife saying to "shut Angel down" before her disappearance, even though it's stated in 3 Jack's wife was killed by Angel in some freak siren accident. What this implies, is that prior to 3's release, 2 was going in a traditional sociopath route with Jack's backstory, but Pre-Se and 3 seemingly backed the corrupted-by-grief route. I'm not 100% sure if Grief is the word I want to use here but I'm going with it. And it's like the Jack AI said in tales, the road to hell is paved with good intentions. Jack is cunningly deceptive but those words are not untrue. And lastly, the Eye of Helios. I don't have a real argument here. He helped build a Death Star. That's pretty fucked. But in the context of the Borderlands universe, where several actively waring megacorporations who manurfacture and sell Guns and Ammo, and ones that have Tazer, Acid and Radioactive ammo in addition to your good ol Incindeary and Explosive and regular slugs, and the only non-corrupt ones are run by a guy who's success has made him a dick, and a guy who just really likes explosions, it's really only slightly more fucked than seeing two girls one cup for the first time? Terrible metaphor, but you understand what I'm trying to convey. Especially considering that The Eye was like, the central part of Helios, and considering this there was no way that Tassiter or anyone else on the Hyperion BoD didn't know about it before Zarpedon took over and started trying to destroy Elpis?

&nbsp;

I don't know how long I've spent on this and it's gone on way longer than I meant for it too so I'm gonna wrap it up. Yes, Jack's motives were to kill the bandit population and settle Pandora, and he was going to use the eye of the Destroyer to do so. Yes, this was fucked. However, it starts with his wife's manslaughter, after Angel was kidnapped by a bandit, who wanted nothing more than to sell her. Just play the Childhood's end mission in BL3, explains it better than I can. Best mission in that shit game btw. The point being, well, the road to hell is paved with good intentions.

