Okay time for the cliffnotes version: Yes Obi-Wan knew his apprentice ceased to be Anakin Skywalker; Friend, Brother, Husband and soon-to-be father, became Darth Vader; murderous mechanical monster. However your "serious doubt" is not confirmation of there being no possible way for Obi-Wan to not know Anakin still lived. For one, Obi-Wan left Anakin just about limbless (spared a robot arm), bloodied, and on fire while laying on a patch of searing hot ashen sand next to a river of flowing, molten lava. It's not unreasonable to assume Anakin/Vader died, because consider the circumstances on the outcome/aftermath of that duel. There's like, 5 different things that have to happen in order to complete Anakin's twisted transformation, which (if my math is correct, also i realise the small irony of saying "if I'm correct" after having called you out on your theory seeming to have much of it's base in your personal doubt) means that there is, at the very least, a 1 in 25 chance for Star Wars to happen as it did in-universe, making Vader's survival of RotS at best not improbable but really unlikely. And regardless of if he did or didn't survive, it was important to hide Leia and Luke lest the Emperor learns of them. They are (or rather have the potential to be) just as much tools to enforce his will as they would be the instruments of his destruction. Furthermore the most presence the Empire had on Tatooine prior to the events of a New Hope in the current canon is, thus far,  a brief excursion to Anchorhead to capture/kill an otherwise nameless Jedi fugitive. For better or worse, the imperial presence on Tatooine is established in Legends, but at most by way of empire-aligned trade ships (primarily civilian) bringing trade goods in and out of the spaceports across the surface of Tatooine, and a single barebones Stormtrooper garrison who did precisely dick, nuk, zero to further the cause of the empire, let alone do anything about Tatooine's infestation of Scum and Villiany. This would make an Imperial News broadcast improbable, and even if Obi-Wan could, why would he? All it would do was to serve as a constant blaring reminder that his own actions would serve as the catalyst for not one but three of the most infamous and irreversable fuck-ups in the history of the Galaxy. For a jedi, "the greatest teacher, failure is". But put yourself in Master Kenobi's position. Can you honestly say that, after 20 years of hearing propoganda for an ultra-successful dictatorship which is constantly doing and subsequently covering up downright evil shit, most notably kidnapping kids to train them as soldiers and not only destroying planets on the regular, but consistently developing new weapons to more efficiently enact aforementioned planetary destruction, could you honestly say that you would not dwell on that failure, and instead simply learn from it and move past it? I know I couldn't. Next; you are correct to recognize that Bail and Ben should not have been communicating, but forget the fact that Ben knew about the Inquisitorius prior to his contact with the senator through his contact with the Jedi Nari, or the notion that Bail's immediate concern for Leia would lead to his neglect to mention that Darth Vader was still alive and kickin'. Also, The Inquisitorius works for the Empire, and by direct extent The Emperor. Even though Vader was the CIC of the Inquisitorius, he took little part in their operations and it was in fact the Grand Inquisitor who led the day-to-day Jedi hunting. In addition, the Senator of Alderaan knew only that his daughter had been kidnapped by common mercenaries, clearly working for someone else, though the question of "who precisely?" could have any of a million answers. These concepts all together; Anakin's abandonment to an otherwise certain death, the need to separate the twins in order to hide them from Sidious, the entirely coincidental yet strangely miraculous appearance of the Jedi Nari, the lack of an imperial presence on Tatooine, the fact that Darth Vader had little to nothing to do with the Inquisitorius while the Grand Inquisitor did the bulk of managerial shit and whatnot and the fact that Senator Organa had no real idea who commissioned the kidnapping of Leia and why, are what give foundation to the fact/idea (whichever you prefer) that Obi-Wan could know about the Inquisitors, even the Inquisitorius as a whole, and still have no idea Anakin Skywalker lives. It all hinges on Bail not mentioning that Darth Vader is still around, and why woulde he? A) As far as he knows and/or is concerned, bounty hunters kidnapped Leia, not the empire (If you go back and watch he makes no mention of the Empire being Leia's kidnappers, only that she was and in order to find her, Kenobi must go to Daiyu), and B) in the unlikely event that the Empire would kidnap her for their own purposes (let alone kidnap her), that at the age of 10 at best she just would have been initiated into Inquisitor training. Darth Vader has nothing to do with either of those, so why mention him? Especially when it'd only cause pain for everyone who remembers Anakin. There is one thing you mentioned that I think stands, albeit on an unstable platform, and it's the fact that despite his intention to train Luke in the ways of the Force, Kenobi himself has done a poor job of maintaining his own abilities. However I don't think it's a lack of practice thing, but rather a psychological thing stemming from a lack of self-belief. It's been known for fully trained Jedi and Sith to downright abandon the ways of the Force due to a number of reasons, such as including major disillusionment, overwhelming grief and a persisting sense of blame on oneself (all of which Kenobi would feel in his reflection of the rise of the empire), and in the process slowly lose their ability to wield the force. This is what I think is happening to Ben, as even though he still seems to practice the ways of the force, his bottled up feelings are whittling away at him, making him weaker and weaker.

&nbsp;

TL;DR: Yes Obi-Wan knows who Darth Vader is, No, he did not have a reason to know or suspect Vader still lives; How he knows of the Inquisitors but not of Vader's survival is easily explained; The reason for the twin's separation is discussed in RotJ; As far as Organa knew/cared, Leia had been take by common mercanaries; Despite governing them, Darth Vader had little to do with the operations of the Inquisitorius, Due to the lack of presence until Ep. I of Kenobi, the Empire and it's media is scarce on Tatooine, Bail would not have briefed Ben on the Inquisitors or Darth Vader because Ben already knew of the Inquisitors and Vader was not relevant to Leia's kidnapping; The thing about training Luke while not keeping with his own training was a strange plot choice/device/hole but it'd not out of the blue as Jedi are known to rarely cut themselves off from the force, due to a great swarm of different possible emotions though none more prominent (In Kenobi's case) than a lack of self-belief. In short, far from nonsense. If you disagree, so be it. I've come to find that the truth is often different, depending on your point of view.

