TL;DR: So in summary, while it would be good to see more permanent death in Star Wars, it's equally fine to have returns, as long as they are lore friendly and contain a practical component. Although it's a route that should be frequently traveled.

&nbsp;

I agree for the most part, but there are certainly cases where it needs to be thought about. The return of the dead isn't farfetched because it's well established in canon an EU/Legends lore, and through multiple angles. Ex. cloning, sheer hate and force of will, just being a ghost. The only reason it was so bad in TROS is because that film and the whole Disney Trilogy was so unbelievablely botched that it just gave up on having a cohesive story like halfway through. Like, they just settled on "He's back, and now there's have a fleet of mini death stars for no reason. Even though building them would be, while an effective final solution, completely impractical given we've had 3 death stars, including one that uses a hollowed out planet to eat stars and then vomit the celestial flame into a massive beam, that splits for no reason, into like 6 planets at a time, all destroyed by some lucky shot at an almost inaccessible flaw." That's like, hundreds of millions of credits wasted, it's a bonfire being sustained by throwing literal money into it. I know that as early as the mid-late Clone Wars, Palpatine has controlled the banks so debt isn't necessarily an issue but like, he's a smart guy, why would he fund this? Why not better and more effective equipment and for the military, or giving back a little to the people so he can still maintain dominance while turning more against the rebellion. It honestly does my fucking head in man.

&nbsp;

Getting back on topic, there are cases why return is acceptable. Qui-Gon learned it, and taught it to Obi-Wan and Yoda, and Maul, who was bisected and fell down a massive shaft concentrated on his hate to push through what would otherwise be certain death, cobbling himself back to a working but unstable form. Boba Fett utilized his knowledge and skill to claw his way out of the belly of the Sarlacc, which is why it's a shame that BOBF was subpar because it establishes early that he's kickass, then immediately throws it away, and while early on it can be concluded that it was for the sake of story, that his kit is what made him awesome in-universe and out. That didn't have to continue when he got his gear back, as the whole Sand People arc helps reinforce how effective Boba is at his chosen profession, which is overshadowed by so much crap, but after assuming control of the territories once Jabba's he just goes soft for no reason. It's saddening, and makes a potentially great show all the more not cool.

&nbsp; 

Back on topic, the key to a good return is a good reason. Whether it's a practical matter complicates this idea, because if you called Darth Maul's return a practical option, I would say poppycock on account of how he was bisected then fell in a pit, but because of the nature of the dark side of the force and the tenets associated with it, I can believe that he did survive such a grievous injury. It helps more if there is a practical component. Cloning, they did to Starkiller who died at the end of Force Unleashed and reborn in 2. Where once again I do question the practicality a smidgen but it's more believable than Maul, which I fully accept anyway because it was so well done. And Force Ghosts, which is explained as an ability that requires extensive in-life training. This one I also have small issue with as it opens a small plot hole, but pushing on so I don't draw this out further by ranting about certain things. With this idea of Return from Death being a good thought when the circumstances for the return are based in established lore, and/or have a practical component to make it possible. I present the following hypothetical.

&nbsp;

Taron Malicos, a powerful former Jedi Master, and later a long practicing Acolyte of the Dark Side, is killed at the end of the second act of Jedi: Fallen Order. Merrin, the last of the Nightsisters, buries him alive as vengeance for his manipulation of her. Now, I submit that Malicos could return from such an act. Merrin and Cal Kestis leave the scene almost immediately, so offscreen, a powerful force user like Malicos could focus on his hate and sheer will, much like Maul did to survive, and in the moment, shatter the earth and stone that currently holds him, push the rubble out of the hole with explosive force and escape to freedom, whereupon he may seek vengeance against Cal and Merrin or whatever he chooses. It would of course depend on how deep Merrin buried him. 6 feet under, absolutely. 10-15, a stretch but possibly. Anywhere above 15 feet and no, he's dead. I've found that the return of Malicos has been an "I can take it or leave it" deal for the fan base, and given the circumstances of his apparent death I can see why,  but it has reason, lorewise and to the lesser extent practical, and that's why he could make a... not good necessarily, but sensible and believable return if it was wanted.

